WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.580
There's never quite been a movie like Avengers:
Endgame.

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:07.680
That's probably because there's never been
a movie franchise like the Marvel Cinematic

00:00:07.689 --> 00:00:08.260
Universe.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:13.400
With over 20 interconnected movies released
over the span of a decade, Endgame had ample

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:18.160
opportunities to make tons of references and
callbacks to a sprawling continuity of characters

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:19.180
and plot twists.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.820
And while many significant lines of dialogue
were pretty easy to spot like

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:24.860
"I can do this all day."

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:26.380
And

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:28.100
"I am Iron Man"

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:32.520
...there were still plenty of other fantastic
callbacks that may have flown over your head.

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:36.220
Here are some of the small details you might've
missed in Avengers: Endgame.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.620
Beware: gigantic, world-ending spoilers ahead.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:45.020
Throughout the film, we got lots of direct
references to previous movies, but some are

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:46.380
less obvious than others.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:50.990
For example, when Natasha Romanoff and Clint
Barton fly their spaceship to Vormir to retrieve

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:55.010
the Soul Stone, one of them quipped to the
other, "we're a long way from Budapest."

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:59.500
That refers back to a mysterious mission the
two of them went on in their shared past that

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.500
they referred to during the events of the
first Avengers film.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.560
"Just like Budapest all over again."

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.400
"You and I remember Budapest very differently."

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:12.100
Meanwhile, just before the un-dusted heroes
all join Cap to fight Thanos and his army

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.160
toward the end of the film, we hear Falcon
jump on the radio to tell his partner: "On

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:16.860
your left."

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.979
That's a callback to when Cap kept lapping
the Falcon when the two first met as competing

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:25.670
joggers at the beginning of Captain America:
The Winter Soldier:

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.240
"On your left."

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:29.580
"On your left."

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:30.480
"Uh-huh.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:31.080
On my left.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:31.880
Got it."

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:33.080
"Don't say it.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:33.580
Don't say it."

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:34.080
"On your left."

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:34.580
"Come on!"

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.860
But the most endearing and heartbreaking callback?

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:41.140
When Happy Hogan chats with Morgan after her
dad's funeral and he asks if she's hungry.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:45.560
She says she wants "cheeseburgers," and Happy
smiles, saying, "Your dad liked cheeseburgers,

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:46.060
too."

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:51.740
That's a reference back to a scene early on
in the very first MCU movie: when Happy and

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:55.860
Pepper pick up Tony after his rescue from
the Ten Rings and he demands two things: that

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.980
Pepper call a press conference…and:

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.820
"I want an american cheeseburger."

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:01.800
Like father, like daughter.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:07.380
Unless you spend a lot of time watching YouTube
videos about the Marvel Cinematic Universe,

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:11.620
there's a better than decent chance that you
have no idea what Avengers: Endgame directors

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:13.520
Joe and Anthony Russo look like.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:18.080
Well, you actually got a great look at one
half of the directing duo you just may not

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:19.260
have realized it at the time.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:23.540
When the movie jumps five years forward in
time, we catch up with Steve Rogers in his

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:25.120
support group in Queens, New York.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:29.940
In that circle of trust is a man who says
he and a date spent the whole evening talking

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:30.460
and crying.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:35.400
That guy is Joe Russo, the younger half of
the directors who also helmed three other

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:41.379
epic MCU entries: Captain America: The Winter
Soldier, Captain America: Civil War, and Avengers:

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:42.080
Infinity War.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.780
This also wasn't Joe Russo's first time in
front of the camera, either.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:51.209
He appeared in an episode of Arrested Development,
a show for which he and Anthony directed several

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:56.260
episodes, as well as in their film You, Me
and Dupree, and all three of their other MCU

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:00.819
films though Joe's part as Bert the paparazzi
in Infinity War hit the cutting room floor

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:02.299
before the movie made it to theaters.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:03.299
"Bert!

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:04.660
I see you Bert!

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:07.460
Bert, you son of a b----.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:08.320
C'mere, Bert!

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:09.160
C'mere!"

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.260
"Man, we gotta get him a girlfriend."

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:15.750
Meanwhile, in addition to directing episodes
of Arrested Development, the Russos also got

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:19.960
behind the lens for several episodes of Dan
Harmon's beloved sitcom Community.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:24.080
And that's why two familiar faces from that
show Ken Jeong and Yvette

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:28.500
Nicole Brown, who played Senor Ben Chang and
Shirley Bennett, respectively appear over

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:29.780
the running time of Endgame.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:34.319
Jeong shows up early, working the security
desk at the storage place containing Ant-Man's

00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:38.689
van, while Brown appears about halfway through
the movie during the jaunt to the SHIELD base

00:03:38.689 --> 00:03:40.000
in the 1970s.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:45.640
As the camera pans past Hank Pym's workbench
at the SHIELD base in the 70s, a distinct

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.800
looking helmet sitting amid the rest of his
stuff that stood out.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:54.880
Longtime comic book fans recognized it immediately
as the original helmet Pym used in the comic

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:56.540
books to chat with his ant buddies.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:58.329
Curious about how the helmet works?

00:03:58.329 --> 00:04:03.329
Lucky you: back in Tales to Astonish #35,
where the helmet first appeared, we got a

00:04:03.329 --> 00:04:07.200
handy dandy diagram explaining all of the
helmet's inner workings.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:11.340
While it stands to reason that a real helmet
made for talking to ants would need something

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:15.840
a lot more sophisticated than "miniaturized
electronic equipment," it was still extremely

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:19.910
cool to see this important piece of Marvel
Comics lore show up on the big screen.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:25.500
Clint Barton's return to the Avengers gave
many fans what they'd wanted ever since Infinity

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:26.000
War.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:30.780
But Endgame also delivered a slyly concealed
reference to the comic book pages that only

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:33.360
the most ardent Marvel fans may have noticed.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:36.970
When Scott Lang is freaking out over doing
the time travel test with the last remaining

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:40.470
Pym Particles, Barton quickly volunteers to
take his place.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:45.190
The moment makes a lot of sense for his character,
as he's got nothing left to lose by this point,

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:49.310
and he's ready to do whatever it takes to
try and satisfy the Avengers' mission to restore

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:49.840
the world.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.660
However, if you've followed Barton's comic
book exploits over the years, you'll know

00:04:53.669 --> 00:04:58.690
that he has a tendency to reach for the Pym
Particles when he wants to change things up.

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:03.860
In Avengers #63, Barton got fed up with not
being able to contribute as much as his superpowered

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:08.580
teammates, so he dropped the Hawkeye schtick,
drank some Pym Particles, and took the name

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:09.180
Goliath.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:14.240
Instead of relying on his superior marksmanship
and fighting abilities, Barton...well, Barton

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.000
got big and smashed stuff.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.180
In a movie as gigantic as Avengers: Endgame,
it'd be truly impossible to catalogue every

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:25.940
single character's appearance let alone even
recognize a fair number of them in the first

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:26.440
place.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:31.300
Case in point: only the most dedicated of
Marvel fans may have recognized Howard Stark's

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:34.140
butler from Steve and Tony's visit to 1970.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:40.150
But loyal viewers of the late, lamented Agent
Carter TV series from 2015 and 2016 would've

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:46.030
recognized Edwin Jarvis, played by James D'Arcy
both on the small screen and in Endgame.

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:50.400
The movie's writing duo of Christopher Markus
and Stephen McFeely were executive producers

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:54.910
on the show, so they probably had a plan to
bring the beloved butler back with this giant

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:57.060
MCU swansong for quite a while.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:02.310
Meanwhile, in a truly blink-and-you'll-miss-it
moment, none other than Howard the Duck joined

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:05.240
all the heroes during the big battle with
Thanos and his army.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:10.080
Keep your eyes trained at the bottom right
of the screen when all the resurrected heroes

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.660
step through Doctor Strange's portals in the
third act specifically when Hope van Dyne

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:15.900
and the Ravagers appear.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.199
If you pay attention, you'll catch little
Howard running onto the battlefield with a

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:21.020
gun in his hands.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:24.860
Believe it or not, this is the third appearance
Howard has made in the MCU.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:28.800
He first showed up in the post-credits scene
for Guardians of the Galaxy, and then appeared

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.620
again on the party planet of Contraxia in
Guardians of the Galaxy vol. 2, chatting up

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:34.620
a nice lady.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:35.979
"You know what they say.

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:38.740
You're out of luck until you've gone duck!

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:40.040
Heh heh heh!"

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:44.060
The phrase "Endgame" appeared twice in previous
Avengers films.

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:48.100
The first time came when Tony Stark was defending
his reasons for building Ultron:

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:49.440
"We're the Avengers.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:56.460
We can bust arms dealers all the live long
day, but that, up there — that's the Endgame."

00:06:56.780 --> 00:07:01.520
Then in Infinity War, Doctor Strange gave
Thanos the Time Stone, a move that no one

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:02.200
could believe.

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:06.680
When Stark asked him why, Strange echoed his
earlier Ultron explanation:

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:09.060
"We're in the Endgame now."

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:15.759
"Endgame," meanwhile, is a phrase that's commonly
used in chess to signify that the game is

00:07:15.759 --> 00:07:20.229
winding down because there aren't very many
pieces left on the board a pretty accurate

00:07:20.229 --> 00:07:23.560
summation when it comes to the status of the
Earth's heroes by the time the film starts

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:24.060
up.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:30.180
However, it might not surprise you to know
that way, way back in 1969, the 71st issue

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:33.289
of The Avengers comic book had a story called
"Endgame."

00:07:33.289 --> 00:07:37.169
The two don't have a ton in common on the
surface: the comic book version features characters

00:07:37.169 --> 00:07:41.819
like the Black Knight and Kang the Conqueror,
two guys who have yet to appear in the MCU

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:42.380
in any form.

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:46.400
But the stories also have more similarities
than you might expect.

00:07:46.419 --> 00:07:51.009
In the comic book, the Avengers fight their
way through time sometimes battling once and

00:07:51.009 --> 00:07:53.040
future members to save the planet.

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:57.880
Is it worth rereading some random comic book
from the late 1960s to get some better insight

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:01.400
on one of the most successful blockbuster
movies of the last ten years?

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:06.900
Probably not...but the similarities do seem
at least a little too close for the name to

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:08.600
be a total coincidence.

